1. What is Hardening?

Hardening is the process of making a Linux system more secure by reducing security risks and protecting it from unauthorized access, attacks, and misuse.

Simply Hardening means locking down your Linux system so that only authorized users can access it and unnecessary services or permissions are removed.

2. Real-Life Example

Imagine you buy a new house.

By default, the house has:

Main door
Back door
Windows
Garage door

If you leave everything open, anyone can enter.

To make your house secure, you:

Lock all doors.
Install CCTV cameras.
Use strong locks.
Allow only family members inside.
Remove duplicate keys.

This process is called hardening.

3.What is Attack Surface?

Attack surface means:

All the possible ways through which an attacker can enter your system.

Example:

If your server has

FTP running
Telnet running
SSH running
Database open to everyone
Weak passwords

Then hackers have many ways to attack.

After hardening,

FTP disabled
Telnet removed
Only SSH enabled
Firewall configured
Strong passwords enforced

4. Linux Hardening Checklist

Linux hardening includes many security tasks.

Keep the system updated
Use strong passwords
Disable unnecessary services
Configure firewall
Disable root login through SSH
Use SSH keys instead of passwords
Set proper file permissions
Remove unused users
Enable logging and monitoring
Regularly apply security patches

5. Let's understand each one.

a) Keep the System Updated

Old software may contain security vulnerabilities.

Attackers often exploit these vulnerabilities.

Update your system regularly.

Ubuntu

sudo apt update
sudo apt upgrade

RHEL
sudo dnf update

Example

Suppose Ubuntu releases a security patch for OpenSSH.

If you don't update,

Hackers may exploit that vulnerability.

b) Disable Unnecessary Services

Every running service consumes memory and may become a security risk.

Check running services
systemctl list-units --type=service

Stop a service
sudo systemctl stop apache2

Disable it permanently
sudo systemctl disable apache2

c) Configure Firewall

A firewall controls incoming and outgoing network traffic.

Example

Allow SSH

Ubuntu (UFW)
sudo ufw allow ssh

Enable firewall
sudo ufw enable

Check status
sudo ufw status

Example

Allow only

SSH (22)
HTTPS (443)

Block everything else.

d) Disable Root Login Through SSH

Logging in directly as the root user is risky.

Instead,

Login as a normal user and use

sudo

Edit

/etc/ssh/sshd_config

Find

PermitRootLogin yes

Change it to

PermitRootLogin no

Restart SSH
sudo systemctl restart ssh

Now,

Hackers cannot directly log in as root through SSH.

e) Enable Logging

Logs help identify problems and security incidents.

Ubuntu

journalctl

Example

See failed SSH logins

journalctl -u ssh

If someone repeatedly tries to log in,

Logs will record those attempts.

f) Apply Security Patches

Companies regularly release security patches.

Install them as soon as possible.

Example

Ubuntu

sudo apt upgrade

g) Disable Unused Ports

Check listening ports

ss -tuln

Example Output

22
80
3306

If MySQL isn't needed,

Stop it

sudo systemctl stop mysql

Note:- Hardening vs Security

Many people think both are the same.

They are different.

Security
Means protecting the system.

Hardening
Means improving the system's security by applying security best practices.

6. What is the Principle of Least Privilege?

Answer:

It means giving users only the minimum permissions required to perform their job.
